A||||3||DreXteR||Need advice, =/||||11||
Z||000000||DreXteR||10-09-2000||01:30 AM||||Oh men!  What a hard time   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Well, 1st the story.  I bougth my fiero about 2 or 3 months. I fix some problems that has, but today a friend came to help me in taking out 3 broked bolts from the manifold. The bolts are so thigth that the extractors broke too.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Well, we decide to take the cilinder heads off and take it to a machine shop to take it off.<P>Now, the advice.<P>Ok, the heads are off and will go to the machine shop. Its wise to rebuild the engine now that its half off. Its a good idea to make it a 3.2? If its so, wich parts are the piks? I looking in a turbo in the future an a possible NOS too, but thats will be in a distant future from now. My friend told me that for a turbo the best compression is 8.5, and that is the fiero 2.8 has. What to do?  The compression in a 3.2 its too high for a turbo? Or should I rebuild with stock crank and pistons? <P>And what performance cam should use? The SI valves from fiero store are a real deal?<P>Thax||||reg||11
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2000||09:23 AM||||Just for thought:<BR>IF you engine was running strong, burned no oil had no knocks, get the heads done, replace timing chain and oil pump to high volumn pump. You may wish to consider a new water pump since the cover is in you hands as well.<BR>If you were not satisified with performance and were burning or leaking oil (espically at the rear main seal) go with a rebuild. That can get rather pricy though.<BR>Presently rebuilding one and have $224 in the block (machine work) $109 in the heads (valve job only) $75 for .30 over pistons,$380 in engine kit w/cam (not stock) I needed a different block $150 and still need to get new clutch @ $110.<BR>I feel like I should have bought a crate motor!||||reg||1
Z||000002||DreXteR||10-09-2000||11:22 PM||||Well, the engine has some power(have some push and response), but have was not top notch anyway. While we dismount parts, more we know about the cheap work that has been done to the engine. The car dont smoke, but leak a little. The pistons look good, but full carboned , push rods are ok.  But if the engine its almost disassabled, why not rebuild it in the rigth way. In that case I dont have to touch it in years.  <P>And if I will do it, why not a performance rebuild!  'Cause I dont think to make this thing again, unless it broke. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||862M4inCA||10-10-2000||05:15 AM||||Remember, if you want to turbo charge that motor it's got to be in good condition...you NEVER want to turbo charge a worn motor since it quickly shortens it's life and you'll be back to debating a rebuild. As for the compression issue, as far as I have known/heard, there isn't really a limit as far as compression goes, just have to make sure your not running boost pressure thats too high (heard to run less than 6 lbs.)unless you install o-ring grooves into the block to help avoid blowing head gaskets when you demand boost from the turbo. But if I was in your shoes, personal opinion, I'd just fix what needs to get fixed, and be done and happy knowing that it's finally back the way it should be. Just my 2 cents anyway.||||reg||1
